---
// fjarr.io landing page — sells the idea, links to the docs.
// Content model + feature-status honesty rule: docs/19-website-and-publishing.md
const capabilities = [
  { icon: "📷", name: "Camera video", desc: "Multi-camera live streams, one encode fanned out to every viewer, adaptive bitrate.", status: "in development" },
  { icon: "🖥️", name: "Remote desktop", desc: "The robot's Ubuntu desktop in your dashboard — unattended access after reboot included.", status: "planned" },
  { icon: "📈", name: "Sensor streaming", desc: "Typed telemetry with change-triggered updates and offline store-and-forward.", status: "planned" },
  { icon: "📦", name: "File transfer", desc: "Resumable, backpressured transfers — a 4 GB log dump over LTE that survives reconnects.", status: "planned" },
  { icon: "⌨️", name: "Remote terminal", desc: "An audited PTY in the browser, over the same session.", status: "planned" },
  { icon: "🔌", name: "Network tunnel", desc: "ssh, scp, a CAN-over-UDP bridge and ros2 topic list straight to one robot behind carrier NAT — the tools you already use.", status: "planned" },
  { icon: "🩺", name: "Fleet observability", desc: "Versions, host metrics, error rates across the fleet.", status: "planned" },
  { icon: "🔄", name: "Atomic OTA", desc: "SWUpdate-based A/B updates with rollback and streamed binary diffs.", status: "planned" },
];
---
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Fjarr — remote connectivity for robot fleets</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Embeddable peer-to-peer connectivity for robots: camera video, remote desktop, files, terminal, a direct network link for ssh and ROS 2, and fleet tooling over WebRTC. Open core, your stack." />
    <style>
      :root { color-scheme: dark; }
      * { box-sizing: border-box; }
      body { margin: 0; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; background: #0d1117; color: #e6edf3; line-height: 1.6; }
      .wrap { max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 24px; }
      header.hero { padding: 96px 0 64px; text-align: center; }
      .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 6vw, 3.6rem); margin: 0 0 8px; }
      .hero .fjarr { color: #58a6ff; }
      .hero p.tag { font-size: 1.25rem; color: #9da7b3; max-width: 46rem; margin: 12px auto 8px; }
      .hero p.sub { color: #7d8590; max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto 32px; }
      .cta { display: inline-block; padding: 12px 28px; border-radius: 8px; font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; margin: 0 8px 8px 0; }
      .cta.primary { background: #2f81f7; color: #fff; }
      .cta.ghost { border: 1px solid #30363d; color: #e6edf3; }
      .banner { background: #161b22; border: 1px solid #30363d; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 20px; color: #9da7b3; text-align: center; }
      section { padding: 48px 0; }
      h2 { font-size: 1.7rem; margin: 0 0 16px; }
      .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; }
      .card { background: #161b22; border: 1px solid #30363d; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; }
      .card h3 { margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 1.05rem; }
      .badge { float: right; font-size: 0.7rem; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 999px; border: 1px solid #30363d; color: #9da7b3; white-space: nowrap; }
      .badge.dev { border-color: #2f81f7; color: #58a6ff; }
      .tiers td { padding: 10px 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid #21262d; }
      table.tiers { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: #161b22; border: 1px solid #30363d; border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; }
      code { background: #21262d; padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; }
      footer { padding: 40px 0 64px; color: #7d8590; text-align: center; }
      a { color: #58a6ff; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="hero wrap">
      <h1>Stop rebuilding robot connectivity.<br /><span class="fjarr">Fjarr</span> is the stack you keep writing.</h1>
      <p class="tag">
        Live camera video, remote desktop, file transfer, telemetry, a remote
        terminal, and an <code>ssh</code>-able link to every robot in your fleet
        — over WebRTC, embedded in <em>your</em> robot, <em>your</em> backend,
        and <em>your</em> dashboard.
      </p>
      <p class="sub">Open-core (AGPL-3.0) libraries you self-host, plus a managed cloud when you'd rather not. <i>Fjärr</i> is Swedish for "remote".</p>
      <a class="cta primary" href="/readme/">Read the docs</a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href="/00-vision/">Why Fjarr exists</a>
      <p style="margin-top: 40px;"></p>
      <div class="banner">
        🚧 Fjarr is being built <b>documentation-first</b> and in the open. The specs
        below are real and current; the code is following them milestone by
        milestone — see the <a href="/17-roadmap/">roadmap</a>.
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="wrap">
      <h2>Three libraries, your stack</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card"><h3>🤖 On the robot</h3><p>Embed <code>libfjarr</code> (C++/GStreamer) into your robot software — or run the <code>fjarr-agent</code> daemon beside it. Hardware H.264 encode, one encoder for any number of viewers.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>🗄️ Next to your backend</h3><p>Deploy the <code>fjarr-server</code> sidecar (78 MB container) next to your existing stack — any language. Your backend mints session grants and receives webhooks. It never touches the media path.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>📊 In your dashboard</h3><p><code>@fjarr/core</code> + <code>@fjarr/react</code> drop robot sessions into the React dashboard you already have. Headless-first components, your design system wins.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap">
      <h2>Capabilities <small style="font-weight:400;color:#7d8590;">— everything is a plugin, including yours</small></h2>
      <div class="grid">
        {capabilities.map((c) => (
          <div class="card">
            <span class={`badge ${c.status === "in development" ? "dev" : ""}`}>{c.status}</span>
            <h3>{c.icon} {c.name}</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;color:#9da7b3;">{c.desc}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="color:#7d8590;">Statuses track the public <a href="/17-roadmap/">roadmap</a> — we don't market what doesn't run.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap">
      <h2>A link to one robot <small style="font-weight:400;color:#7d8590;">— not a VPN</small></h2>
      <p style="color:#9da7b3;max-width:52rem;">
        Every robot company keeps a list of things they occasionally need to do to
        a robot that nobody will ever write a protocol for. The network tunnel is
        the escape hatch that stops that list becoming a roadmap: an authorized
        operator gets a routable address for <b>one</b> robot, inside the session
        that already carries its video. <code>ssh</code> in. <code>scp</code> the
        log out. Point a UDP bridge at the CAN bus. Run
        <code>ros2 topic list</code> and see the robot's graph from your laptop.
      </p>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card"><h3>🔒 Isolation you can't misconfigure</h3><p style="margin:0;color:#9da7b3;">Connect to two robots at once and they still can't see each other. Every link ends at you and nothing is forwarded between them — there's no path to leave open by mistake.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>🎫 One set of permissions</h3><p style="margin:0;color:#9da7b3;">You already decide who may reach robot 42. The tunnel is that same decision, in the same token, in the same audit log. No second identity system to run alongside the one you have.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>⏱️ Access that expires</h3><p style="margin:0;color:#9da7b3;">The link exists while a session does, then it's gone. Nothing becomes permanently reachable, and there's no extra daemon on the robot to install, update or license.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p style="color:#7d8590;">
        It deliberately isn't a mesh: no shared network, no robot-to-robot routing, no
        always-on reachability. It's developer and support access — read
        <a href="/27-network-tunnel/">the design</a> for exactly where that line sits.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap">
      <h2>Open core, honest split</h2>
      <table class="tiers">
        <tr><td><b>Free &amp; open (AGPL-3.0)</b></td><td>All three libraries, the signaling sidecar, the protocol, the docs, the demos. Genuinely usable alone, self-hosted forever.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Fjarr Cloud</b></td><td>Managed signaling + TURN, fleet observability, staged OTA campaigns, SSO — metered by robots, session-minutes, and relay bandwidth. Same contract as self-hosting: switch either way, no rework.</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p style="color:#7d8590;">The whole business plan is a public document: <a href="/03-product-strategy/">product strategy</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <footer class="wrap">
      Fjarr · <a href="/readme/">documentation</a> · built documentation-first in Sweden 🇸🇪
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
